**Definition.** A unit test checks the smallest testable piece of code (a method or class) in isolation, automatically, and reports pass or fail. It gives fast feedback, protects against regressions when you refactor, and documents intended behaviour. Unit tests sit at the base of the test pyramid; integration tests check components together, and end-to-end tests check the whole system.

**Good unit tests (FIRST):** Fast, Independent (no shared state or order dependence), Repeatable (deterministic), Self-validating (assertions, not manual inspection), Timely (written with or before the code).

**How to write one.** Use Arrange, Act, Assert; test one behaviour per test and give it a descriptive name. Cover the normal case, boundaries (empty, one element, maximum), invalid input and error paths.

```java
class LotterySystemTest {
    @Test
    void addReturnsFalseForDuplicate() {
        LotterySystem lot = new LotterySystem();          // arrange
        assertTrue(lot.addParticipant(1));                 // act + assert
        assertFalse(lot.addParticipant(1));
    }
    @Test
    void randomPickOnEmptyReturnsMinusOne() {
        assertEquals(-1, new LotterySystem().randomPick());
    }
    @Test
    void removedParticipantIsNeverPicked() {
        LotterySystem lot = new LotterySystem();
        lot.addParticipant(1); lot.addParticipant(2); lot.removeParticipant(1);
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) assertEquals(2, lot.randomPick());
    }
}
```

**Testing randomness.** Inject the random source (a seeded `Random` or a fake) so tests are deterministic; for fairness, run many picks and check each id appears with roughly equal frequency, with a loose tolerance, or just assert that every pick is a current member.

**Isolation.** If a class calls a database or network, depend on an interface and use a mock or stub (Mockito) so the test checks only your logic.

**Also:** run tests in CI on every commit, aim for meaningful coverage (high coverage does not prove correctness), add a regression test for each bug you fix, and consider property-based tests for operations like add and remove.
